// **The target operating system, at compile time** (ADR-0180) — `os()`, folded in sema to a
// `Basic.Operating_System` member. Before this the compiler had **no notion of an OS at all**: no `#if`,
// no `OS` constant, no build setting a library could read, and its whole notion of a target was
// `TargetLayout`'s two numbers. `modules/Time` carried a comment about `CLOCK_MONOTONIC :: 6` being a
// macOS-only value it could not guard.
//
// What is pinned, each an independent bit of the exit code:
//
//   * **`os()` in a body**, compared against a member of the library enum;
//   * **`os()` at file scope**, through a `#run` whose callee reads it — the established idiom
//     (`modules/Random`'s `GOLDEN :: #run golden_seed();`);
//   * **`os()` directly in a file-scope constant**, which was E0230 *"a name failed to resolve at file
//     scope"* until ADR-0180 §3: the signature phase folded the call and threw the value away, so
//     `jr-mir`'s thunk found nothing and resolved `os` as a name;
//   * **`size_of` in a file-scope constant of a builtin type**, which was E0201 *"unresolved name
//     `s64`"* until ADR-0180 §4 — a *different* cause, and the more interesting one: resolution walks
//     the top-level expression arena **flat**, so it visited `s64` as an expression in its own right
//     before ever reaching the call that makes it a type argument;
//   * **`size_of` of a struct in a file-scope constant**, the gap `Window` and `Socket` both documented
//     and worked around by moving the check into a procedure;
//   * **`switch` over the enum is exhaustive**, which is the whole reason `os()` answers an enum rather
//     than an integer: "this program does not handle Windows" is a compile error, not a wrong branch.
//
// **The exit code is the sum on this machine's OS**, so the file cannot assert one number on every host.
// Each bit is a claim that is true on all three, and the bit worth 1 says which host it ran on: 1 for
// macOS, 2 for Linux, 4 for Windows — so a reader of a failure knows what the total should have been.
#import "Basic";

HERE :: os();
BUILTIN_SIZE :: size_of(s64);
POINT_SIZE :: size_of(Point);
VIA_RUN :: #run os_code();

Point :: struct {
    x: s64;
    y: s64;
}

os_code :: () -> s64 {
    if os() == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return 1;
    }
    if os() == Operating_System.LINUX {
        return 2;
    }
    return 4;
}

// A `switch` over the enum, which is exhaustiveness-checked (ADR-0067). Its answer must agree with
// `os_code`'s chain of `if`s — two spellings of one decision, so a compiler that folded `os()` to two
// different values in two places would be caught.
os_code_switched :: () -> s64 {
    switch os() {
        case .MACOS;
            return 1;
        case .LINUX;
            return 2;
        case .WINDOWS;
            return 4;
    }
    return 0;
}

main :: () {
    n := 0;

    // Which host, as the low bits: 1, 2 or 4.
    n = n + os_code();

    // The same answer through a `#run` at file scope, and through a `switch`.
    if VIA_RUN == os_code() {
        n = n + 8;
    }
    if os_code_switched() == os_code() {
        n = n + 16;
    }
    // `os()` read straight into a file-scope constant, with no `#run` around it.
    if HERE == os() {
        n = n + 32;
    }
    // `size_of` in a file-scope constant: a builtin, and a struct.
    if BUILTIN_SIZE == 8 {
        n = n + 64;
    }
    if POINT_SIZE == 16 {
        n = n + 128;
    }

    exit(n);
}
